WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.990
- The Lion King is out,
and as of this taping,

00:00:02.990 --> 00:00:07.240
is having a tricky time feeling
all the love at 60% fresh.

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:08.520
The reviews are still trickling in,

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.300
and really could go
either way at this point,

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.430
but the trek up this
rock is feeling steeper

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:14.210
than expected, something the last two

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:17.050
Disney live-action films
have also experienced.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:21.690
Dumbo at 46% rotten, and
Aladdin at 57% rotten.

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:25.300
Could we be facing Disney
live-action fatigue?

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:27.790
Hi everyone, I'm Mark Ellis,
welcome to the After Show.

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:30.390
Yes, I was saying
live-action in air quotes.

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:33.270
So happy to welcome you all
to this particular discussion.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:37.040
If you have not yet seen the
new Lion King, fair warning,

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:40.680
this is a spoiler-heavy
discussion, so be prepared.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:43.600
I am so excited to welcome
some A-List members

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:45.470
of the pride here to the chat.

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:47.950
Ladies and gentlemen, she
is a Rotten Tomatoes editor,

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:50.450
Miss Jacqueline Coley's here.
- How y'all doin'?

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:51.934
- Oh, we get applause
today from the crew too.

00:00:51.934 --> 00:00:52.767
(chuckling)
- I know.

00:00:52.767 --> 00:00:54.744
- And she's Geek Bomb host Maude Garrett.

00:00:54.744 --> 00:00:56.820
- Hey y'all, what's up?
- In a very, uh,

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:59.117
seasonal I guess for the
moment for the discussion.

00:00:59.117 --> 00:01:00.511
- Why did Maude not get applause?

00:01:00.511 --> 00:01:01.799
Let's give Maude some applause.

00:01:01.799 --> 00:01:03.403
(applauding)
(giggles)

00:01:03.403 --> 00:01:05.440
- Well we saved the best
round of applause for last

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:06.810
because we're in the
presence of greatness,

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:09.810
ladies and gentlemen, Rotten
Tomatoes editor-in-chief,

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:13.390
the one the only, Mr.
Joel Meares is here today!

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:15.370
Oh, this is so exciting
to have all y'all here.

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:17.830
So what we do on this show is we like

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:19.430
to talk about the movie in-depth,

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:21.250
but before we get into that discussion,

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:22.960
I do wanna just pitch it to each panelist

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:24.503
really quick, is this
movie fresh or rotten?

00:01:24.503 --> 00:01:26.020
Because I go into this thinkin',

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:27.790
oh well it's Disney, it's The Lion King,

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:29.180
of course it's gonna be fresh.

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:31.080
My opinion is that it's
a very fresh movie,

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:32.940
I enjoyed myself start to finish.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:36.500
Jacqueline Coley, am I crazy?
- Fresh!

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:38.930
This injustice will not
stand about this movie

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:41.140
not being fresh as far as I'm concerned.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:43.930
I literally brought out my tinfoil hat

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:45.900
this weekend 'cause
it's a grand conspiracy.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.170
This is a good movie, y'all,
don't let the score fool you.

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:49.193
- All right, Maude, do
you agree with that,

00:01:49.193 --> 00:01:52.552
is this a fresh, based on
your shirt I'm gonna say yes.

00:01:52.552 --> 00:01:54.180
(singing Lion King theme)

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:57.720
- Yeah, it's fresh as, I
think that not only has

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:01.670
the look of this movie matured,
but so has my take on it.

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:03.100
Because I'm seeing and watching this movie

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:06.440
from a whole different
perspective, of the adult.

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:09.020
That's all I'm tryin' to say.
- Okay, now Joel, you are also

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:11.538
an adult as far as we
know, but you're also

00:02:11.538 --> 00:02:12.371
- This is true.
- Is that right?

00:02:12.371 --> 00:02:13.230
- the editor-in-chief of Rotten Tomatoes

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:14.730
and we're kinda looking at it right now,

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:16.870
this thing is barely clinging to fresh,

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:18.753
this is like Mufasa on
the edge of the cliff.

00:02:18.753 --> 00:02:19.851
- Don't you dare, how dare you.

00:02:19.851 --> 00:02:21.680
- And one Scar review could
come along and knock it off.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:23.430
Do you think this is a fresh movie?

00:02:24.442 --> 00:02:27.280
- Fresh-ish (chuckles), yeah, so I think

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:29.863
I understand why the
score is where it's at.

00:02:30.889 --> 00:02:33.230
I enjoyed this film for what it is,

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:36.050
I think it's beautiful, there
is a lot to recommend it.

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:39.170
I don't disagree with
anything that's been said

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:40.740
so far, although I'm sure I'll disagree

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:43.220
in a few minutes with
some things that are said.

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:45.900
But I didn't feel anything, and I was left

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:48.210
a little cold by this Lion King.

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:49.250
And I'm not a heartless person,

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:50.320
I will cry at the drop of a hat,

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:52.691
that Subway commercial,
it had me in tears.

00:02:52.691 --> 00:02:56.160
But this film didn't quite get
to the Subway level for me.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:57.600
- You couldn't feel the love tonight?

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:00.730
- I didn't feel the love,
I felt a little lack.

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:02.650
- I'm so happy Maude made that pun for me,

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:04.090
'cause I'm already done with my quota

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:06.140
of Lion King gags today, so Joel,

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:07.410
I wanna stick with you for a minute

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:08.860
with our first topic which is,

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:11.300
are you surprised that the movie is right

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:14.440
at that level of could be
fresh, could be rotten,

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:15.470
because again, this thing comes out,

00:03:15.470 --> 00:03:18.430
and even regardless of what
I felt about the movie,

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:19.490
just seeing all the ads for it

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:21.770
and seeing the excitement
that fans had for it,

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:22.880
you would expect this thing to be

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:25.500
up in the 80s or 90s, right?
- I was surprised.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:26.740
I don't know that I
was expecting the score

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:28.190
to be that high, but the audience

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:30.860
I saw it with certainly
was into this film.

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:32.290
I was tellin' the guys earlier,

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:33.920
the woman next to me was singing along,

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:36.311
fist-pumping, which made my experience

00:03:36.311 --> 00:03:38.032
- I'm right here, how dare you.

00:03:38.032 --> 00:03:39.826
- somewhat awkward (laughs), exactly.

00:03:39.826 --> 00:03:41.990
She's sitting next to me
now, on the Australian couch.

00:03:41.990 --> 00:03:45.360
No I think, I was
surprised that it is so low

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.790
to be honest, I was expecting
it to be slightly higher.

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:49.660
But we have to understand that this is,

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:51.010
for many people going to see this movie,

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:53.230
particularly people who
do this for a living,

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:54.547
and have to think about the industry a lot

00:03:54.547 --> 00:03:57.490
and think about the
state of culture and art,

00:03:57.490 --> 00:03:58.720
they're going and seeing this not just

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:00.853
as The Lion King, or The Lion King 2019,

00:04:00.853 --> 00:04:02.387
they're seeing this as The Lion King,

00:04:02.387 --> 00:04:05.710
the 10th live-action remake from Disney

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:07.460
since Alice in Wonderland.
- Yes.

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:09.700
- And so this film
opens with a huge amount

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:12.710
of baggage because
people are really divided

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:13.980
over what Disney's doing at the moment

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:15.250
with these live-action remakes.

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:17.280
And the whole concept, the whole nature

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:18.980
of this film is divisive
for certain people,

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:21.970
and that discussion is really
bleeding into the reviews

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:23.715
and the takes that people
have with this film.

00:04:23.715 --> 00:04:28.320
I'm surprised that it's not
in the 70s or maybe low 80s,

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:31.160
but not overly surprised that there is

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:32.860
something of a backlash coming through.

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:34.420
- Yeah, Maude, I agree with Joel's point

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:36.430
that I think that a lot
of it is just people's

00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:38.710
commentary on should we be having

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:41.060
these live-action remakes,
so the score being

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:42.460
as low as it is, is that something

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:44.850
that kinda surprised you?
- Oh, big time!

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:45.990
And I don't really think that it's fair

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:49.780
to a movie, a stand-alone
movie, to own and wear

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:52.210
the baggage of its
predecessors and the decisions

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:54.560
that Disney's making as
a company on the whole.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:58.060
I think same sort of thing in the cinema

00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:00.250
for the world premiere everyone's cheering

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:03.830
and applauding at the end of
every scene, let alone act.

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:06.580
It was such a great atmosphere in there,

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:10.230
I was singing every word,
(all laughing)

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:13.890
which, whatever, but
I absolutely loved it.

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:15.330
And I thought that it looked brilliant,

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:18.340
the story was there,
they kept it authentic

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:21.110
to what the original was whilst
adding new elements to it,

00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:22.440
and I thought that that really worked.

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:24.510
Timon and Pumbaa stole the show for me,

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:28.900
I thought that that duo and
that upgrade was fantastic.

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:30.880
It's not a perfect film,
but I absolutely enjoyed it.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:31.930
- Yeah, Jacqueline, I think that that's

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:33.430
the other question that people have is,

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:35.740
well is this gonna be
as good as the original,

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:37.840
simply because we've seen the original,

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:40.530
and Maude's able to sing with every song.

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:43.320
Maybe it didn't do enough
differently to win critics over,

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:45.430
is that why you see that score so low?

00:05:45.430 --> 00:05:46.840
- I have a hard time
with it, and especially

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:48.980
with the critic community
that so often sees

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:52.200
a foreign language entry and
then an English language remake

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:54.450
and can equally like the two of those,

00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:55.730
that's the way I kinda looked at this.

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:58.860
I looked at this as, this
is a different language,

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:01.720
meaning the medium of
computer hyperrealistic

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:04.330
photorealistic images,
that is a different medium

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:07.550
to T animations, you're
going to lose things

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:09.640
by going into this next
medium, and you are

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:11.930
gonna gain things by going
into this next medium.

00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:14.840
I just think when you
balance the two together,

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:16.980
that's where you kinda
have to basically leave it

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:20.010
where it is, and I do think
this has a ton of upgrades.

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:22.360
I don't know where it sits in the ledger

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:25.410
against the masterpiece
that the first one is,

00:06:25.410 --> 00:06:27.240
I don't think it's fair
to compare the two.

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:29.690
However I think there was
a lot of care being placed

00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:33.360
in this, Jon Favreau put a lot of respect

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:35.690
to the original and a lot of deference

00:06:35.690 --> 00:06:37.900
to the filmmakers and also to Julie Taymor

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:39.890
for what she did in the stage play.

00:06:39.890 --> 00:06:42.420
This is not just an adaptation
of the animated classic,

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:44.630
it's also an adaptation of the stage play,

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:47.580
and infusing that in there,
for one is a huge upgrade.

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:51.060
The Nala character's
storyline is a huge upgrade,

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:52.300
regardless of what some of y'all may think

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:54.540
about Beyonce, yes I'm
a Stan, and so as far

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:56.190
as I'm concerned, the
movie is a masterpiece

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:57.880
because it has Beyonce, yes I'll admit it.

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:00.670
However, that doesn't
mean I can't acknowledge

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:03.590
the fact that Beyonce's a great singer,

00:07:03.590 --> 00:07:05.270
maybe not the best voice actor,

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:07.260
you know what I'm sayin',
even I can admit that.

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:08.210
- You're really stirring the Hive there.

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:10.337
- I know, the Hive is gonna come for me.

00:07:10.337 --> 00:07:12.359
- The comments, direct them
at Jacqueline Coley please.

00:07:12.359 --> 00:07:14.640
- I love her, trust me, she's great,

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:16.340
but I do think when you put her

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:17.850
alongside someone like Billy Eichner,

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:21.500
who is a tried-and-true
actor, he does this,

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:24.090
he does voice acting, he
literally does improv.

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:26.130
You can't compare the two,
they're separate roles,

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:27.540
but you can't compare.
- Right, well,

00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:29.620
this might be the easiest
spoiler we've ever done

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:31.460
because if you've seen
the original Lion King,

00:07:31.460 --> 00:07:34.020
you know what's going
to happen in this movie,

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:36.800
and it's not shot-for-shot,
but it is beat-for-beat.

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:38.230
The big difference, obviously, being

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:40.830
the photorealistic CGI, it's not even

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:42.220
performance or motion capture, I mean,

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:45.170
this is rendered by artists, and I think

00:07:45.170 --> 00:07:46.660
that that being the big difference,

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:49.990
Maude, is this live-action
remake, is it taking

00:07:49.990 --> 00:07:54.350
enough risks, or do you think
that it's supposed to stay

00:07:54.350 --> 00:07:56.790
with the source material
for this particular film?

00:07:56.790 --> 00:07:58.423
- I think that this is the
movie where they can do that,

00:07:58.423 --> 00:08:00.870
only because it's not
actors having their own

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:03.730
creative input, having their
own creative license with it.

00:08:03.730 --> 00:08:06.300
They have absolute control
over how this looks.

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:08.305
I think the problem that
you're finding here is

00:08:08.305 --> 00:08:10.930
that the choice to make it
as realistic as possible

00:08:10.930 --> 00:08:15.870
whilst trying to retain the
emotions of the characters,

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:18.100
that's where it's falling
short a little bit.

00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:20.170
I mean, that beautiful
scene where Simba first eats

00:08:20.170 --> 00:08:23.240
the grubs, and it has this (gags) moment,

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:25.170
you're not gonna see a
realistic lion do that

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:26.750
because it cannot physically do that,

00:08:26.750 --> 00:08:29.440
it doesn't have that emotion,
and another little gripe

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:32.180
that I have, and I'm so sorry for this,

00:08:32.180 --> 00:08:36.190
but if you're gonna make
a realistic-looking lion

00:08:36.190 --> 00:08:40.830
cub and hold it up, and
there's no male parts.

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:43.195
- Oh, you wanted to see more, um,

00:08:43.195 --> 00:08:44.100
(Joel laughs)
more nudity, I guess.

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:45.330
- But this is the thing where it's like

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:46.880
you're making this
choice, but then you have

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:49.590
to figure out where to draw the line,

00:08:49.590 --> 00:08:52.220
ha ha, lion, aah!
- Oh my God.

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:53.090
- Sorry!
- So I would say

00:08:53.090 --> 00:08:55.508
that they drew the line
correctly with the holding up

00:08:55.508 --> 00:08:56.370
of Simba.
- Yeah I think so,

00:08:56.370 --> 00:08:57.570
I missed a lot about this film,

00:08:57.570 --> 00:09:00.027
lion peen was not one of those things.

00:09:01.161 --> 00:09:04.488
- No, no, you have to take
a moment for lion peen.

00:09:04.488 --> 00:09:05.357
- Let's have a (Jacqueline's
laughter drowns him out).

00:09:05.357 --> 00:09:07.300
But I actually don't personally feel

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:08.430
like they did take enough risks.

00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:09.920
Jacqueline, you mentioned the stage show,

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:12.650
I didn't see the stage show
in this production at all,

00:09:12.650 --> 00:09:14.915
and I felt they could've
really drawn from that.

00:09:14.915 --> 00:09:16.770
They had a lot of beautiful
music in that stage show,

00:09:16.770 --> 00:09:18.380
there's a lot of more
development that happens

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:21.190
in that stage show, and I
don't think it was integrated.

00:09:21.190 --> 00:09:24.180
I felt that Favreau, who I
agree did a beautiful job

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:27.383
of directing and putting
together this replica,

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:29.560
I don't think they drew enough,

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:31.620
and I think the Nala storyline, again,

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:33.040
I don't think it was expanded enough,

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:34.250
I think if you look at what they did

00:09:34.250 --> 00:09:37.030
with Jasmine in Aladdin,
that felt different to me

00:09:37.030 --> 00:09:38.780
from the original characterization.

00:09:38.780 --> 00:09:41.010
This Nala, they gave
her a bit more creeping

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:42.630
around the rock with Scar and stuff,

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:44.000
but they didn't take it nearly as far

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:45.930
as they could have taken
it to give us something

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:48.740
that was worth revisiting, I feel.

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:50.960
- You hear the term cash grab
thrown around sometimes too

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:53.690
because everybody knows
if you do this level

00:09:53.690 --> 00:09:57.020
of CGI and it looks as
realistic as it did,

00:09:57.020 --> 00:09:59.850
I think that it did further
that evolution forward,

00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:01.660
but I think that people are going to say,

00:10:01.660 --> 00:10:02.850
well it's The Lion
King, of course you knew

00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:04.610
you were gonna make boatloads
of money doin' this.

00:10:04.610 --> 00:10:06.840
And we've had three
Disney live-action remakes

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:08.830
in the last four months, with Dumbo,

00:10:08.830 --> 00:10:10.980
and then Aladdin, and now The Lion King,

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:14.290
so is the term cash grab fair, Jacqueline?

00:10:14.290 --> 00:10:16.180
- I mean, yeah, but
so's any movie that goes

00:10:16.180 --> 00:10:19.380
to the box office for a certain idea.

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:21.150
They don't put these
out 'cause they love us,

00:10:21.150 --> 00:10:22.800
they put them out to make money.

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:25.640
I'm so sorry, if you
didn't guess that by now,

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:27.620
I don't know, maybe you've
missed the whole box office.

00:10:27.620 --> 00:10:28.553
- Are you saying it's a business?

00:10:28.553 --> 00:10:31.020
- Yeah, it's a business, and so yes,

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:34.180
I think Disney is making
a strategic decision,

00:10:34.180 --> 00:10:36.600
however you can still do that with care,

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:38.220
with integrity, you can still do it

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:40.590
with a reverence and a
love for the original.

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:43.040
You can do both, you
can say I'm doing this

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:45.310
to make money, maybe win some awards,

00:10:45.310 --> 00:10:47.430
and also because I care and wanna give

00:10:47.430 --> 00:10:49.865
this new version to a new audience.

00:10:49.865 --> 00:10:53.380
I think you can do both, and
it doesn't necessarily...

00:10:53.380 --> 00:10:56.380
Why did we make Endgame,
they wanted it to make money.

00:10:56.380 --> 00:10:59.140
So I get why people are angry about it,

00:10:59.140 --> 00:11:01.560
I do think they're picking
the wrong properties

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:03.390
to revisit, in my personal opinion.

00:11:03.390 --> 00:11:04.383
- Well that's interesting
you bring that up

00:11:04.383 --> 00:11:06.430
because those movies that I referenced

00:11:06.430 --> 00:11:08.430
that have already come
out, Dumbo and Aladdin,

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:10.350
both, like I mentioned
at the top of the show,

00:11:10.350 --> 00:11:13.730
not as well-received as
when Cinderella came out.

00:11:13.730 --> 00:11:15.620
Everybody's like, oh
great, because there's

00:11:15.620 --> 00:11:17.590
a great graphic that Rotten Tomatoes did

00:11:17.590 --> 00:11:20.270
where you see the lineage
of Disney live-action films

00:11:20.270 --> 00:11:23.540
as far back as the 1994
Jungle Book, which is fresh.

00:11:23.540 --> 00:11:25.300
And then we got a little
rotten for a while,

00:11:25.300 --> 00:11:28.180
and then we came back with
the film of The Jungle Book

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:30.510
with all the animals that
Jon Favreau had done,

00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:32.850
Cinderella, the Alice in
Wonderlands take it down

00:11:32.850 --> 00:11:35.540
a little bit and so it's been
through peaks and valleys,

00:11:35.540 --> 00:11:37.490
but Maude it seems like
we're almost going down

00:11:37.490 --> 00:11:40.337
another valley at this point.
- Big time, I think

00:11:40.337 --> 00:11:42.750
there is such thing as
inundation, and I think

00:11:42.750 --> 00:11:44.750
that when you're not even
letting things mellow,

00:11:44.750 --> 00:11:46.530
we had the same thing
with Star Wars, I feel,

00:11:46.530 --> 00:11:48.530
where that was just trying
to push out as many movies

00:11:48.530 --> 00:11:51.050
as possible because it
was making so much money,

00:11:51.050 --> 00:11:53.520
by the time we got to
Solo, it was just like,

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:55.570
I'm not even enjoying this ride anymore.

00:11:55.570 --> 00:11:57.356
There is too much of a good
thing that can take place.

00:11:57.356 --> 00:11:58.970
- That's my defense of Solo,
is it's not a cash grab

00:11:58.970 --> 00:12:00.690
because it didn't grab that much cash!

00:12:00.690 --> 00:12:01.840
- That's it, they burnt it out,

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:04.340
there was just powder
coming out of the cash cow.

00:12:04.340 --> 00:12:06.480
So I think yeah, if we just slow the roll

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:07.800
a little bit, what's the rush,

00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:09.560
why do we have to have it all so fast?

00:12:09.560 --> 00:12:11.390
And you're exactly right,
there are some stories

00:12:11.390 --> 00:12:13.960
that don't need retelling,
and Dumbo is one of them.

00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:15.790
Let's talk about the
elephant that was captured

00:12:15.790 --> 00:12:18.422
and made to perform for
people's entertainment.

00:12:18.422 --> 00:12:20.910
Ah, that was not fun to watch.

00:12:20.910 --> 00:12:23.410
- PETA loved that one, but wait a minute,

00:12:23.410 --> 00:12:24.810
so your point though about I guess

00:12:24.810 --> 00:12:26.650
this whole lineage of the live action

00:12:26.650 --> 00:12:28.780
and how many they keep going, for a studio

00:12:28.780 --> 00:12:31.370
that has Marvel, that
is literally giving you

00:12:31.370 --> 00:12:33.910
the blueprint for how to slow roll

00:12:33.910 --> 00:12:36.780
to a grand crescendo,
there are two side studios,

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:39.540
being Lucas Film and Disney proper,

00:12:39.540 --> 00:12:41.654
they literally do not follow that formula

00:12:41.654 --> 00:12:43.390
- Figure it out, yeah.
- at all,

00:12:43.390 --> 00:12:45.360
it's all pedal to the metal, it gives me

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:48.070
a new reverence for Kevin Feige's strategy

00:12:48.070 --> 00:12:51.170
and how he's able to maybe push back.

00:12:51.170 --> 00:12:52.330
- Well something that Kevin Feige said,

00:12:52.330 --> 00:12:54.210
I interviewed him recently
and what was amazing was

00:12:54.210 --> 00:12:58.110
that he only thinks in five
and then ten year increments.

00:12:58.110 --> 00:12:59.990
So he's not thinking about
how we can get this out

00:12:59.990 --> 00:13:01.990
month-to-month, it's like really he thinks

00:13:01.990 --> 00:13:05.340
in long-term strategies and
I think that that's so smart.

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:07.250
- Yeah, and I wanna get
into what we're gonna

00:13:07.250 --> 00:13:09.757
be seeing from Disney
live-action coming up.

00:13:09.757 --> 00:13:11.540
Mulan, we just saw the trailer for that,

00:13:11.540 --> 00:13:12.890
that's gonna be a little
bit later in the show,

00:13:12.890 --> 00:13:14.070
but for right now, let's move on

00:13:14.070 --> 00:13:16.080
to the next topic, and Jacqueline,

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:18.430
I just wanna ask you
about the highs and lows

00:13:18.430 --> 00:13:20.478
of this movie, what did you think about,

00:13:20.478 --> 00:13:21.311
- The highs and lows.
- what were your

00:13:21.311 --> 00:13:23.523
favorite parts, what was maybe not,

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:26.333
more of a meow than a roar for you?

00:13:27.230 --> 00:13:29.650
- Actually it's interesting,
one of the highs was

00:13:29.650 --> 00:13:32.780
the Timon and Pumbaa
characters, their relationship.

00:13:32.780 --> 00:13:35.230
Billy Eichner is definitely
going to break out

00:13:35.230 --> 00:13:37.270
from this, I really feel he got to shine

00:13:37.270 --> 00:13:39.360
when Seth Rogan talks
about Billy in the movie,

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:41.800
he's like, that's just
him, they captured him.

00:13:41.800 --> 00:13:44.590
And what those guys could
do in that booth is really

00:13:44.590 --> 00:13:47.770
magical, and I think more people
should pay attention to it.

00:13:47.770 --> 00:13:50.263
The lows for me, Be Prepared.

00:13:51.390 --> 00:13:53.790
That was a (laughter
drowns her out), huge miss.

00:13:54.920 --> 00:13:57.140
- Joel, you are nodding
your head in agreement?

00:13:57.140 --> 00:13:59.890
- Yeah, I think it's a beat poet rendition

00:13:59.890 --> 00:14:03.640
of a thrilling Broadway song
that doesn't quite land.

00:14:03.640 --> 00:14:06.110
I will say on the Be Prepared front,

00:14:06.110 --> 00:14:08.740
could you imagine, though, a full version

00:14:08.740 --> 00:14:11.710
like the original, 'cause I
think that would just get weird,

00:14:11.710 --> 00:14:14.410
with those weird-faced
hyenas sort of singing along?

00:14:14.410 --> 00:14:16.410
I don't know that they kind of,

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:17.957
maybe they should have
just snipped it altogether.

00:14:17.957 --> 00:14:20.290
- That's the thing, the rumor
was they were gonna cut it,

00:14:20.290 --> 00:14:21.910
and in my personal
opinion, they should have,

00:14:21.910 --> 00:14:23.480
they should've made a Braveheart speech,

00:14:23.480 --> 00:14:25.970
which I think was maybe their idea.

00:14:25.970 --> 00:14:27.895
- An anti-Braveheart speech.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah,

00:14:27.895 --> 00:14:29.440
or an anti-Braveheart
speech, you know what I mean,

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:31.884
like we're going to do this
and it's terrible, and have--

00:14:31.884 --> 00:14:32.717
- We're going to take their freedom!

00:14:32.717 --> 00:14:34.240
- Yeah, have that rousing moment,

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:36.030
but don't put it to song.
- Eat some zebras and shit?

00:14:36.030 --> 00:14:38.660
- Yeah, yeah, but this is the thing.

00:14:38.660 --> 00:14:42.550
Jeremy Irons' entire
character in the '94 Lion King

00:14:42.550 --> 00:14:43.420
is camp.
- Yes.

00:14:43.420 --> 00:14:44.730
- For all those people at the Met Gala

00:14:44.730 --> 00:14:47.190
that didn't understand
the definition of camp,

00:14:47.190 --> 00:14:50.350
Jeremy Irons in The Lion King as Scar.

00:14:50.350 --> 00:14:53.220
The movie's hyperrealistic
and gritty and dramatic,

00:14:53.220 --> 00:14:55.140
camp and realistic are literally

00:14:55.140 --> 00:14:58.040
the antithesis of each other, so I get

00:14:58.040 --> 00:14:59.680
why they should have cut it out,

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:01.000
and I get why they wanted to.

00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:02.300
- Though I think they
achieved some of that

00:15:02.300 --> 00:15:03.570
with Timon and Pumbaa.
- Yes!

00:15:03.570 --> 00:15:04.620
- Which is interesting 'cause those

00:15:04.620 --> 00:15:06.160
two characters were able to go there,

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:08.700
but I think their overall
characterization of Scar

00:15:09.697 --> 00:15:12.890
as this darker, more serious character,

00:15:12.890 --> 00:15:14.150
wouldn't allow them to
do it, and I would say,

00:15:14.150 --> 00:15:16.410
actually, overall
despite Be Prepared being

00:15:16.410 --> 00:15:18.760
a bit of a fizzer, Scar himself was a bit

00:15:18.760 --> 00:15:19.810
of a highlight for me.
- Yes!

00:15:19.810 --> 00:15:23.200
- I felt that Chiwetel did a beautiful job

00:15:23.200 --> 00:15:26.060
voicing that character, I
do feel that more than Nala,

00:15:26.060 --> 00:15:29.550
the Scar-Sarabi relationship
was actually one

00:15:29.550 --> 00:15:32.440
that I liked seeing fleshed
out and felt added a dimension.

00:15:32.440 --> 00:15:35.170
Alfre Woodard, also a
high, but you're right,

00:15:35.170 --> 00:15:38.070
the only, not the only
reason, but the big reason

00:15:38.070 --> 00:15:40.170
to see this film is Timon and Pumbaa

00:15:40.170 --> 00:15:43.800
and Billy Eichner, I mean,
he's gay and fabulous meerkat,

00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:45.680
- Yeah.
- I can't get enough.

00:15:45.680 --> 00:15:47.110
- [Jacqueline] And
Emmy-nominated as of this morning

00:15:47.110 --> 00:15:48.633
for Billy (Joel drowns her out).

00:15:48.633 --> 00:15:49.682
- [Joel] Yes, congratulations, Billy.

00:15:49.682 --> 00:15:52.230
- As soon as Timon and Pumbaa show up,

00:15:52.230 --> 00:15:55.440
'cause I love The Lion King, the 1994 one,

00:15:55.440 --> 00:15:58.270
but I love it primarily
because of the relationship

00:15:58.270 --> 00:16:01.930
with Mufasa and Simba, so that first act

00:16:01.930 --> 00:16:04.010
in the 1994 version is what really drew me

00:16:04.010 --> 00:16:06.510
to it, Maude, but seeing
this, I would have to agree

00:16:06.510 --> 00:16:07.900
that I thought Timon and
Pumbaa were highlights,

00:16:07.900 --> 00:16:10.480
but I also thought that the hyenas,

00:16:10.480 --> 00:16:12.520
voiced by Keegan-Michael
Key and Eric Andre,

00:16:12.520 --> 00:16:14.230
they really made me laugh a lot too.

00:16:14.230 --> 00:16:16.660
- I just like that there
was a new take on it.

00:16:16.660 --> 00:16:18.930
We could have done
play-by-play, word-for-word,

00:16:18.930 --> 00:16:20.730
and they chose not to do that, and I liked

00:16:20.730 --> 00:16:23.350
that different take that they had on it.

00:16:23.350 --> 00:16:24.610
I think there's a reason why they had

00:16:24.610 --> 00:16:26.870
to change one of the hyenas as well.

00:16:26.870 --> 00:16:30.770
Which was a good thing,
I'm gonna have to agree

00:16:30.770 --> 00:16:33.460
that Chiwetel Ejiofor's
take on Scar was great.

00:16:33.460 --> 00:16:36.410
I love Jeremy Irons's version,
but I think it's like,

00:16:36.410 --> 00:16:39.630
instead of trying to mimic
or to do exactly the same,

00:16:39.630 --> 00:16:41.560
it doesn't work, make
your own version of it,

00:16:41.560 --> 00:16:43.130
and some of the characters ran with that,

00:16:43.130 --> 00:16:45.760
and some of the characters really faulted

00:16:45.760 --> 00:16:49.100
trying to do that, and I
think that Scar was great,

00:16:49.100 --> 00:16:52.660
I think it needs to be
camp, and I disagree

00:16:52.660 --> 00:16:55.620
with having camp and
realistic don't mesh together.

00:16:55.620 --> 00:16:58.010
I think when you look at the live-action

00:16:58.010 --> 00:16:59.900
Beauty and the Beast, I
think that when you had

00:16:59.900 --> 00:17:03.710
every scene with Luke Evans and Josh Gad,

00:17:03.710 --> 00:17:06.410
they went full camp, and
they were the best parts

00:17:06.410 --> 00:17:08.270
of that movie, and then
Emma Watson played it

00:17:08.270 --> 00:17:10.230
a little bit safe and
didn't go camp at all,

00:17:10.230 --> 00:17:13.239
and that juxtaposition
that you had, the synergy

00:17:13.239 --> 00:17:15.310
didn't sit there, and
that's what you're having

00:17:15.310 --> 00:17:17.030
in this movie, where
Chiwetel's bringing it,

00:17:17.030 --> 00:17:19.030
Timon and Pumbaa are going extra camp,

00:17:19.030 --> 00:17:21.970
and then you're having
Nala and adult Simba

00:17:21.970 --> 00:17:24.250
playing it very realistic and very safe,

00:17:24.250 --> 00:17:25.780
and they're not on the same page.

00:17:25.780 --> 00:17:26.613
- That's what's interesting to me is

00:17:26.613 --> 00:17:27.990
that we're talking about
the highlights right now

00:17:27.990 --> 00:17:30.110
and I have not heard
Donald Glover, Beyonce yet

00:17:30.110 --> 00:17:33.090
because I felt, I didn't
think that they were bad

00:17:33.090 --> 00:17:34.220
by any stretch of the imagination,

00:17:34.220 --> 00:17:36.200
but I just felt like I didn't feel

00:17:36.200 --> 00:17:38.910
any emotional tinges
with their relationship

00:17:38.910 --> 00:17:41.670
or even in their individual
vocal performances.

00:17:41.670 --> 00:17:44.540
I thought the performance of
Can You Feel the Love Tonight

00:17:44.540 --> 00:17:46.130
played very well, I know
a lot of people were upset

00:17:46.130 --> 00:17:48.710
that it was during the day,
but I like the update on it.

00:17:48.710 --> 00:17:50.560
It's not gonna do what the
Elton John song does to me,

00:17:50.560 --> 00:17:52.580
but I thought it was fine.
- It was getting there,

00:17:52.580 --> 00:17:55.470
and then it stopped, I
was like, oh, I'm in this,

00:17:55.470 --> 00:17:57.387
and then they went, they
cut out the instruments,

00:17:57.387 --> 00:17:59.510
and it sort of crescendoed
and then it just ended,

00:17:59.510 --> 00:18:00.801
- She licked his face, and
that's enough for the kids.

00:18:00.801 --> 00:18:02.147
- and I was like, oh I
could have done some more.

00:18:02.147 --> 00:18:03.560
(Maude drowning out Joel)
- There was chemistry!

00:18:03.560 --> 00:18:05.280
- But I'm like, they have chemistry,

00:18:05.280 --> 00:18:09.510
I bought Donald Glover a
lot more as this sort of

00:18:09.510 --> 00:18:13.020
slacker Simba than I did as the noble

00:18:14.110 --> 00:18:17.060
he lives in me kind of
Simba, I definitely liked

00:18:17.060 --> 00:18:18.620
that aspect of his performance when he got

00:18:18.620 --> 00:18:21.070
with Nala and went back to
save the pride, et cetera,

00:18:21.070 --> 00:18:22.100
I was a little bit less enthused.

00:18:22.100 --> 00:18:23.680
- I couldn't understand some of his words.

00:18:23.680 --> 00:18:26.673
He's like, (mumbling),
I'm like you don't what?

00:18:26.673 --> 00:18:28.510
What was that--
- He's got mumble music, so...

00:18:28.510 --> 00:18:29.343
- [Joel] You don't need to know the words,

00:18:29.343 --> 00:18:32.640
they're the same as the previous
film, every single line.

00:18:32.640 --> 00:18:36.140
- Stop, also he's mumble as a rapper,

00:18:36.140 --> 00:18:38.620
so I'm not surprised he's
mumble as a voice actor either.

00:18:38.620 --> 00:18:40.490
So that doesn't bother
me, but in the original,

00:18:40.490 --> 00:18:43.780
the side characters are
just as more interesting.

00:18:43.780 --> 00:18:45.930
The side character's always
the most interesting part.

00:18:45.930 --> 00:18:47.640
Scar was the most
interesting in the first one,

00:18:47.640 --> 00:18:50.860
and so were Timon and Pumbaa,
so they just kept that over.

00:18:50.860 --> 00:18:54.810
I'm okay with Nala and Simba
not being as interesting,

00:18:54.810 --> 00:18:56.610
'cause let's be honest,
they weren't to begin with.

00:18:56.610 --> 00:18:58.410
- There's highs and lows with this movie,

00:18:58.410 --> 00:18:59.920
and then there's one
that I'm still confused

00:18:59.920 --> 00:19:01.650
in my own head, I'm tryin' to rationalize,

00:19:01.650 --> 00:19:04.130
and that would be, the hair
scene where the giraffe eats it

00:19:04.130 --> 00:19:06.200
and then we, then the
giraffe, and I'm like,

00:19:06.200 --> 00:19:07.780
I'm watchin' the hair blow, and I'm like,

00:19:07.780 --> 00:19:09.397
oh great, it's gonna get back to Rafiki

00:19:09.397 --> 00:19:11.420
and then he's gonna know, but man,

00:19:11.420 --> 00:19:14.230
does that hair take an
interesting off-ramp

00:19:14.230 --> 00:19:16.500
through a giraffe's intestinal system!

00:19:16.500 --> 00:19:18.846
- It went through the circle of life,

00:19:18.846 --> 00:19:21.010
that was the whole point.
- It's the circle of life.

00:19:21.010 --> 00:19:23.330
- Very literal, I found
that it was kind of

00:19:23.330 --> 00:19:26.320
like a Forrest Gump
feather situation mixed

00:19:26.320 --> 00:19:28.300
with just some strangeness for about,

00:19:28.300 --> 00:19:30.140
what, 17 minutes or something?

00:19:30.140 --> 00:19:32.247
I was like, we get it,
I know what this is.

00:19:32.247 --> 00:19:34.060
- But in Forrest Gump,
you never see anybody eat

00:19:34.060 --> 00:19:35.670
the feathers then sittin' on the john.

00:19:35.670 --> 00:19:37.970
- No, but it coulda been
in those chocolates.

00:19:37.970 --> 00:19:40.960
- You're seeing hair
remove itself from dung

00:19:40.960 --> 00:19:42.900
and continue to, the dung beetle was cute,

00:19:42.900 --> 00:19:45.240
but it's like, I thought
that was interesting.

00:19:45.240 --> 00:19:46.340
I'm not ready to say it was bad

00:19:46.340 --> 00:19:47.826
or good, but it was interesting.

00:19:47.826 --> 00:19:49.976
- This is how it goes,
the animal (mumbles).

00:19:49.976 --> 00:19:51.399
- I will say it was the biggest difference

00:19:51.399 --> 00:19:53.210
